WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:01.860
- [Siti] Food insecurity

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.830
is a real problem in different families.

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.270
This is something that I had
to go through growing up,

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.760
and so I didn't want anyone

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.224
to ever have to spend a night
hungry and not have food.

00:00:14.224 --> 00:00:16.460
(lively music)

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:20.219
- [Narrator] This Great Big
Story is by Singapore Airlines.

00:00:20.219 --> 00:00:22.802
(lively music)

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:29.380
- My name is Siti Noor Mastura,

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.230
and I am the founder of Back2Basics.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:35.450
Back2Basics is a nonprofit
organization, and what we do

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.970
is that monthly we serve
families with groceries.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.320
Food insecurity was an
issue for our family.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.340
Food became a struggle.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.500
Hunger is something so awful,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.610
and you don't think about it
until you're in that situation.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.540
You don't think you will
end up in that situation.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.110
So in 2013, so I actually
started volunteering

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.520
with different organizations
with my best friend,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:57.840
and so that's when I
started talking to her

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.707
and saying, "You know what,
we should do something

00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:02.087
"about the food
insecurities in Singapore."

00:01:03.604 --> 00:01:04.950
(upbeat music)

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:07.600
So the first groceries that we bought

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.120
were basically with our own money,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.930
and we delivered it to three houses,

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.090
and then it just grew into more families,

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.310
and that's what we did
for the next six years.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.710
The next drive that's
happening, it's 31 families.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.760
We have those who are elderly.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.810
We have big families.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.820
We have single parents.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.730
So it's rice, oil, broccoli, onions,

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.150
carrots, potatoes, bananas.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.330
Our idea is that

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.840
if you're not spending money
buying these basic things,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.070
that money can be rolled out

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:41.770
into something else that you need.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:49.250
I don't think that poverty
will end in my lifetime,

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.140
but I think the point is in knowing

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.510
that it's not about
solving everyone's issue.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.540
It's really about making a difference,

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.040
even in just one person's life.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.050
And in order to just solve
one person's problem,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:03.930
it really just takes one person,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.280
and that person can be you.

